#!/bin/sh
# Every implementation cites a repository, a paper or an online source, or says
# plainly that it was invented. The rule was set by the operator on 2026-09-20
# after it was skipped twice in one session, and a skill and a memory both failed
# to prevent that, because both depend on being remembered. This does not.
#
# A commit that adds or changes Python must carry, in its message, either:
#   * a source -- a URL with or without its scheme, such as
#     stackoverflow.com/q/39417091, or an arXiv id such as arXiv:2604.21570; or
#   * the literal token INVENTED: followed by what was searched for and why
#     nothing was usable.
#
# This is a commit-msg hook, not pre-commit, and the distinction is the whole
# reason it works: git runs pre-commit BEFORE the message exists and passes it no
# arguments, so a pre-commit hook cannot read the message and every commit looks
# uncited. The first version of this file was a pre-commit hook and blocked a
# commit that cited three papers; it passed its own test only because the test
# handed it a path git never supplies. commit-msg receives the message file as $1.
#
# To bypass for a genuine non-implementation commit (a rename, a typo, a revert):
#   git commit --no-verify
set -eu

changed=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM | grep -E '\.py$' || true)
[ -n "$changed" ] || exit 0

msg=$(cat "$1" 2>/dev/null || true)
# A merge or a revert is not an implementation.
case "$msg" in
  Merge*|Revert*) exit 0 ;;
esac

# A bare host and path counts, because that is how this repository actually cites:
# stackoverflow.com/q/39417091 and unicode.org/Public/UCD/... are the committed
# spelling, and the scheme is left off in prose throughout. The first version of
# this line demanded https:// and refused a message citing four sources for that
# reason alone, which teaches writing https:// rather than looking anything up.
# The TLD list grew on 2026-09-21 for the same reason in miniature: it refused a
# citation of tcl.tk, Tk's own manual, because .tk was not on it. A gate that
# rejects the canonical source for the thing being fixed is worse than none.
if printf '%s' "$msg" | grep -Eqi \
  'https?://|arxiv[:. ]?[0-9]{4}\.[0-9]{4,5}|[A-Za-z0-9-]+\.(com|org|net|io|dev|edu|gov|ai|tk|uk|de|ch|nl|fr|jp|se|it|info|sh|to)/[^ ]'; then
  exit 0
fi
if printf '%s' "$msg" | grep -q 'INVENTED:'; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  commit-msg: this commit changes Python and cites nothing.

  Python touched:
MSG
printf '    %s\n' $changed >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  Before writing code, look for a repository, a paper or an online source that
  already solves it. Then put the source in the commit message:

      Fix the loop-invariant alignment (arXiv:2604.21570, github.com/owner/repo)

  If you searched and nothing exists, say so and say what you searched for:

      INVENTED: searched for prior art on tiered oracle evidence in program
      verification; the nearest analogue is SLSA's manifest-vs-attestation
      distinction, from a different domain, so this code is ours.

  A rename, a typo fix or a revert is not an implementation: git commit --no-verify

MSG
exit 1
